3322b962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435467 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435467 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435467 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435467 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e4932278-fd35-5668-a02a-427a9d1bc8b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435470 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435470 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435470 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435470 #edb/ref 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the lines $y=2x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3M}{2}x.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435470 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3441cc98-9576-5e74-bdad-b0f5fae54049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435470 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{3M}{2}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}x{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{4}+\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{14}{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{[\\frac{14}{15}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{14}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435472 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435472 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163610" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435472 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435472 #edb/ref 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,3\\le x\\le 5,\\,\\,2\\le y\\le 4\\}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435472 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f4fdbea-b5e1-5c4f-b10b-d88e66b48be2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435472 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{5}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}(\\frac{125}{3}-9)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{98}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{[\\frac{98}{3}y]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{49M}{6}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{49M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{y}^{2}=\\frac{49}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{3}}=\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444155 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444155 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444155 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444155 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444155 #edb/ref 1